--TEST--
An ordering whose operand the checker could not type is decided by that operand's runtime tag — `rule:types/arithmetic`'s rows where the table has one, and a catchable throw carrying `E0715`'s own wording where it has none (`rule:types/arithmetic`, `rule:types/erased-member-access`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/arithmetic`'s ordering table is closed, and where the static types name a
// row `nvs-codegen` emits the machine comparison. A `mixed` or a union names
// none, so the *tag* names it instead — `nvs_ir::Helper::ValueLt` and its two
// siblings — and the same table's completeness becomes a throw rather than a
// diagnostic, because the tags are where it first became answerable.
final class Ask {
    // One place where both operands are `mixed`, so every row below is the
    // tag-dispatched one and not a statically typed comparison in disguise.
    public static function lt(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            if ($a < $b) {
                return "lt";
            }
            return "no";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws: " . $e->message;
        }
    }

    public static function ltEq(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            if ($a <= $b) {
                return "le";
            }
            return "no";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws";
        }
    }

    public static function gt(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            if ($a > $b) {
                return "gt";
            }
            return "no";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws";
        }
    }

    public static function gtEq(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            if ($a >= $b) {
                return "ge";
            }
            return "no";
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws";
        }
    }

    public static function cmp(mixed $a, mixed $b): string {
        try {
            return ($a <=> $b) as string;
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            return "throws";
        }
    }
}

final class Point {
    public int $x = 1;
}

// The numeric rows, across the three representations the table spans. Each is
// exact over the whole range of both operands, which is why an integer beside
// a `float` is a comparison rather than a widening: § 4's own closing
// paragraph says so, and the widening would throw above 2^53.
echo Ask::lt(5, 7), " ", Ask::lt(7, 5), " ", Ask::lt(5, 5), "\n";
echo Ask::lt(2.5, 5), " ", Ask::gt(2.5, 5), "\n";
uint $big = 18446744073709551615;
// The second pair is the one a widening would get wrong: `2^53 + 1` and the
// `float` `2^53` are two different numbers, and reading the integer as an
// `f64` first would round them together and answer `le`.
echo Ask::lt(9223372036854775807, $big), " ", Ask::ltEq(9007199254740993, 9007199254740992.0), "\n";

// `rule:types/arithmetic`'s `bool` row: the ordering of the one bit it already is.
echo Ask::lt(false, true), " ", Ask::lt(true, false), " ", Ask::ltEq(true, true), "\n";

// `<=>` is the same row read whole rather than asked one question.
echo Ask::cmp(5, 7), Ask::cmp(7, 5), Ask::cmp(5, 5), Ask::cmp(2.5, 5), "\n";

// A `NaN` operand is unordered against everything including itself, so all
// four ordering operators are false and `<=>` is `1` — PHP's answers, and the
// reason these helpers take an ordering rather than a `bool` from the row.
float $nan = Core\Math::NAN;
echo Ask::lt($nan, 1), Ask::gt($nan, 1), Ask::ltEq($nan, $nan), Ask::gtEq($nan, 1), "\n";
echo Ask::cmp($nan, 1), " ", Ask::cmp(1, $nan), "\n";

// The union a division produces is the shape that reaches this without any
// `mixed` written at all: `int / int` is `int|float` (§ 4), which erases to the
// same tagged representation and takes the same helper.
int $n = 7;
int $d = 2;
if (($n / $d) < 4) {
    echo "division orders\n";
}

// The other end of the closed table. Each of these is refused where it is
// *written* when the static types show it (`E0715`); behind a `mixed` the
// refusal is exactly as real and arrives as a catchable throw instead.
echo Ask::lt("a", "b"), "\n";
echo Ask::lt(1, "b"), "\n";
echo Ask::lt(null, 1), "\n";
echo Ask::lt(new Point(), new Point()), "\n";
array<int> $xs = [1, 2];
echo Ask::lt($xs, $xs), "\n";

// The refusal is the operator's, not the operand's: the same two strings
// compare for equality with no throw at all (`rule:expressions/equality-semantics`), and `<=`, `>`,
// `>=` and `<=>` agree with `<` about having no row.
mixed $s = "a";
mixed $t = "b";
if ($s == $t) {
    echo "BAD\n";
} else {
    echo "equality still answers\n";
}
echo Ask::ltEq($s, $t), Ask::gt($s, $t), Ask::gtEq($s, $t), " ", Ask::cmp($s, $t), "\n";

// Agreement: the tag-dispatched answer is the statically typed one, asked of
// the same values through both spellings. Counted rather than read off a line,
// so a row that grew its own comparison fails here.
int $agreed = 0;
int $i = 0;
while ($i < 4) {
    if (Ask::lt($i, 2) == "lt") {
        if ($i < 2) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    } else {
        if ($i >= 2) {
            $agreed = $agreed + 1;
        }
    }
    $i = $i + 1;
}
echo "agreed ", $agreed as string, "/4\n";
--EXPECT--
lt no no
lt no
lt no
lt no le
-110-1
nononono
1 1
division orders
throws: no ordering for a `string` against a `string` — `Core\Str::compare` is the ordering two strings have
throws: no ordering for a `int` against a `string`
throws: no ordering for a `null` against a `int`
throws: no ordering for a `object` against a `object` — ordering two objects needs the `Comparable` class named where the comparison is written (`rule:classes/comparable`)
throws: no ordering for a `array<T>` against a `array<T>`
equality still answers
throwsthrowsthrows throws
agreed 4/4
